          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is on four heart-failure medicines, and her ankles are swelling again. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about those drug classes?",
          "answer": "**If your mother has chest pain or discomfort, call emergency services now. Do not drive her yourself.** Chest discomfort can be a sign of a heart attack, and treatment works best in the first minutes. A heart attack can also feel like breathlessness, unusual tiredness, nausea, or discomfort in the jaw, back, or upper belly, so treat those the same way.\n\nIf she has no chest pain, the ankle swelling on its own is not an emergency by these rules, but it still needs prompt contact with her heart team. Call emergency services for breathlessness at rest or breathlessness that comes on suddenly. Also call if she cannot speak in full sentences, has blue or grey lips or fingertips, or is drowsy or confused while struggling to breathe. The research summary also lists breathlessness when lying flat, fainting, and new confusion as reasons to get urgent help. I could not find guidance on which weight gain or urine changes should prompt a call, so ask her heart team for those thresholds.\n\nFor heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (an ejection fraction of 40% or below), the guidance describes four \"foundational\" drug classes, usually started together or in quick succession:\n\n1. **Renin-angiotensin system blockers:** an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI) where suitable, otherwise an ACE inhibitor or an angiotensin receptor blocker.\n2. **A beta-blocker** with evidence in heart failure. It is started when the person is not congested.\n3. **A mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist**, with potassium and kidney function monitored.\n4. **An SGLT-2 inhibitor**, used for heart failure whether or not the person has diabetes. It is the newest of these classes and has also shown benefit in preserved ejection fraction.\n\nDoses are increased gradually as blood pressure, kidney function and potassium allow. I cannot give dose details. The cautions for SGLT-2 inhibitors that I found come from diabetes guidance: genital infections, fluid loss, and, rarely, ketoacidosis even when blood sugar is not high.\n\nThe guidance I found does not say how strong the evidence is for each recommendation, so I cannot rate that. I found no disagreement between authorities about these four classes.\n\nDo not change or stop any medicine without speaking to her prescriber. When you contact her team, ask whether the swelling means fluid is building up, which of her four medicines are being adjusted right now, and what blood tests are due.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
